\subsection{Dual space}
The Dual space was invented to decipher a specific type of operator, $A: L \to \mathbb{K}$ in which $\mathbb{K}$ is any sum of scalars. 
\begin{definition}[Dual Space]
    If $L$ has finite dimension, then $L$'s dual space is defined as 
    $$L^* := \mathcal{L}(L, \mathbb{K}) = \set{f \mid f: L \to \mathbb{K}, f(\b{x}+\b{y}) = f(\b{x}) + f(\b{y}), f(k\b{x}) = kf(\b{x})}.$$
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Infinite-Dimensional linearity \& Dual Space]
    In the history of studying linear functions on infinite-dimensional vector spaces, an impressive idea was that for many \emph{continuous linear functionals}, especially in Hilbert spaces such as $L^2,$ $\b{x}(t)$ can be represented in the prototypical form
    \begin{equation*}
        f_{\phi}(\b{x}) := \int_a^b \phi(t) \b{x}(t) dt.
    \end{equation*}
    
    For linear space $L = \set{\b{x}: \b{x} \in C^1([a,b]), \b{x}(a) = \b{x}(b) = 0},$ \emph{Paul Dirac} introduced Dirac $\delta,$ which stemmed from the generalized cutoff function $H = \mathbf{1}_{\g 0},$ initially lacking proper definition of derivative. However, if we use the prototypical function, $$f_{\phi'}(\b{x}) := \int_a^b \b{x}(t) d \phi(t) = \left. \b{x}(t)\phi(t) \right|_a^b - f_{\phi}(\b{x}') = -f_{\phi}(\b{x}')$$ whenever $\phi$ is differentiable. We define the Dirac distribution $\delta_{t_0}$ through $f_{\delta}(\b{x}) = -f_H(\b{x}'),$ and thus, in the distributional sense, $\delta_{t_0} = H'.$

    Now, by Dirac $\delta$ function, $\forall t_0 \in [a,b],$ $$f_{\delta}(\b{x}) = -f_{H}(\b{x}') = -\int_a^b H(t - t_0) d\b{x}(t) = - \int_{t_0}^b d\b{x}(t) = \b{x}(t_0).$$ The $\delta$ marks the value of $\b{x}$ in one variable.
    
    The Dirac $\delta$ reminds me the smooth cutoff function in my \textit{Exponential Sum} Proofs, $\eta(t).$ 
    \begin{equation*}
        \eta(x) = \left\{ \begin{array}{rcl}
            0 & \mbox{for} & x > C \\
            1 & \mbox{for} & x < -C \\
            C^\infty(\mathbb R) & \mbox{for} & x \in [-C,C]
        \end{array} \right..
    \end{equation*} 
    As well as the supporting function $\psi,\, \exists \text{ constants } A < B, \; \psi(u) \neq 0 \Leftrightarrow u \in [A,B]; \quad \psi \in C^\infty((A,B)).$
    
    The similarities expose in ways they operates, though one is distributional while the other is smooth. The utility of both functional Dirac $\delta$ and $\eta, \psi$ is to serve as \emph{weight} in the integral. 

    From the \emph{weight} perspective, we consider Dirac $\delta$ a non-continuous, singular-point-only weight that only allows one point to pass; while $\eta, \psi$ are absolutely smooth for which they return roughly sum(integration) on certain interval to reduce the error from Fourier transform.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Dual Basis]
    For a base $\b{e}_1, \b{e}_2, \cdots, \b{e}_n \in L,$ its dual basis are vectors $\b{f}_1, \b{f}_2, \cdots, \b{f}_n \in L^*,$ such that $$\b{f}_i(\b{e}_j) = \delta_{ij}.$$ $\delta_{ij}$ is \emph{Kronecker delta}. 
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
    If vector space $L$ has a finite dimension, $\dim L = \dim L^*.$
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We are prone to prove that the Dual basis in the previous definition is an actual base in the dual space. 

$\forall \b{f} \in L^*, \b{x} \in L,$ for a base $\b{e}_1, \b{e}_2, \cdots, \b{e}_n \in L,$
\begin{align*}
    \b{f}(\b{x}) &= a_1\b{f}(\b{e}_1) + a_2\b{f}(\b{e}_2) + \cdots + a_2\b{f}(\b{e}_n) \\
    &= a_1 b_1 + a_2 b_2 + \cdots + a_n b_n \quad(\text{for some constants } b).
\end{align*}
Incorporating Dual basis, 
\begin{align*}
    \b{f}_{i}(\b{x}) &= a_1\b{f}_i(\b{e}_1) + a_2\b{f}_i(\b{e}_2) + \cdots + a_2\b{f}_i(\b{e}_n) \\
    &= a_i \\
    \Longrightarrow \b{f}(\b{x}) &= b_1 \b{f}_1(\b{x}) + b_2 \b{f}_2(\b{x}) + \cdots + b_n \b{f}_n(\b{x}) \iff \b{f} = b_1 \b{f}_1 + b_2 \b{f}_2 + \cdots + b_n \b{f}_n.
\end{align*}

If $\b{f} = 0,$ then $\forall \b{x} \in L, \b{f}(\b{x}) = 0,$ which only happens when $b_1, b_2, \cdots, b_n$ are all $0.$ Thus, $\b{f}_1, \b{f}_2, \cdots, \b{f}_n$ are linear independent. Plus, $\forall \b{f} \in L^*,$ $\b{f}$ can be represented by linear combination of a dual basis. Hence, any dual basis is an actual basis, $\dim L = \dim L^*.$
\end{proof}
\begin{corollary}
    If vector space $L$ has a finite dimension, $L \cong L^*.$ However, if we are considering infinitely dimensional cases, $L$ and $L^*$ are not necessarily isomorphic.
\end{corollary}

